WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.525 --> 00:00:04.150
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: Welcome back.

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:07.670
Today we're incredibly lucky to be joined by Rene Almeling.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:12.360
Renee is a professor of Sociology and Public Health at Yale University

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.500
and she's one of the world's leading experts

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.930
on the market for sperm and egg.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.610
Rene, thank you so much for joining us.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:19.780
DR. RENE ALMELING: It's a pleasure to be here.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.488
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: So Rene, you're an expert on the buying

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:26.760
and selling of sperm and egg, sometimes also referred to as gametes.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.190
And we're going to talk a little bit about those two markets.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.680
And we're going to start by talking just a bit about sperm.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.604
So tell me a little bit, who's the typical sperm donor in America?

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:40.270
DR. RENE ALMELING: The average sperm donor is a college student.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.840
He's in his early 20s.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.490
He's interested IN making a little bit of extra money

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.620
at a job that doesn't take too much of his time.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.454
When I did interviews with sperm donors, I

00:00:49.454 --> 00:00:51.120
found that there were really two groups.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.600
There was this group of sort of younger college students.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.810
There was also a group of older men who had

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.900
known somebody who experienced trouble getting pregnant or infertility.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.090
And so some of those folks had sort of put in some of their energy

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:04.760
into being sperm donors as well.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.676
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And a lot of this is from your amazing book,

00:01:07.676 --> 00:01:11.490
Sex Cells, C-E-L-L-S, which I think is one of the best titles I've seen

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.450
on the academic market.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:16.080
How much is the typical sperm donor paid in America?

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.680
DR. RENE ALMELING: Well, the average payment is about $100, $125 per sample.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.970
What most people don't know about sperm banks

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.680
is that men go through an extensive screening process.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.310
And then they're going in at least once a week for six months to a year

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.830
in order to earn that $100 to $125 per sample.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.350
So we have this idea that men can wake up one morning

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.350
and say, oh, I'm going to go donate sperm this morning, and go make $50.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:40.960
And that's not really how it works.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.800
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And so the screening screens out a lot of men.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.560
Any sense about how many men get screened out?

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.500
I have to say for my class, when I taught this in a bricks and mortar

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.450
class once, I tried to become a sperm donor.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.242
I was screened out.

00:01:51.242 --> 00:01:52.574
I don't know that says about me.

00:01:52.574 --> 00:01:54.250
Am I normal to have been screened out?

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.262
What kind of number of people get screened out?

00:01:56.262 --> 00:01:58.470
DR. RENE ALMELING: Well, you're in very good company.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.178
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: Thank goodness.

00:02:00.178 --> 00:02:03.290
DR. RENE ALMELING: So about 99% of men who apply to become sperm donors

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:04.240
will be screened out.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.470
So sperm banks are looking for men who are extremely

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.139
fertile with very high sperm count.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.980
So if you look at that sort of bell curve of fertility,

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.360
they're looking for men in the top 2% to 3% of sperm count.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.590
That's because they freeze the sperm or cryopreserve it.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:20.780
That kills off about half the cells.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:23.614
So that's right there knocking out most men who apply.

00:02:23.614 --> 00:02:25.530
And then the men who make it through that test

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.780
need three generations of their family health history to look pretty good.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:33.520
So men who have a lot of heart attacks or mental illness

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.077
or alcoholism in their family won't make it through the screening process.

00:02:37.077 --> 00:02:39.160
And then they have to be willing to go in at least

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.280
once a week for a year to make donations.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.017
And so you know if you have 100 men who make that first call to the sperm bank,

00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:47.600
you're really walking out the door with about 1% of the people

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.900
making it through that process.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.920
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: So there's a lot of screening that happens.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.450
And I know you've written your work about how men are paid.

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:56.033
So just because I donate sperm, does that mean I automatically

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:57.400
get paid for that donation?

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:58.816
DR. RENE ALMELING: No, it doesn't.

00:02:58.816 --> 00:03:01.890
So in sperm banks, men go in and they make this donation.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.070
If their sperm count is high enough to meet the bank's standards,

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.900
then they will be paid for that donation.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.350
But all kinds of things can cause that sperm count to drop, including

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.340
being too stressed out, say by your finals in school.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:17.410
If you're drinking too much alcohol, if you're not getting enough rest,

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:20.640
if you're sick, so all of these things can cause sperm count to drop.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.030
So men's sperm count, which-- another thing that people don't realize

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:27.880
is men's sperm count really fluctuates over days and months.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.930
And so sperm donors have to stay pretty healthy

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.460
and take good care of themselves in order to actually make

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.870
that money from being a donor.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.590
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And there's also an abstinence requirement

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.550
as I understand it.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:37.508
DR. RENE ALMELING: Yes.

00:03:37.508 --> 00:03:41.610
So men who donate sperm because they need to build up that sperm count,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:46.080
have to be abstinent from any sort of sexual activity for about 48 hours

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.460
before they go donate.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:51.270
So then you can start to understand how sperm donors find this a little bit

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:51.870
onerous.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.260
They have to schedule their sex lives.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.750
They have to be mindful of their diet and their exercise.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.980
And so if they're going in to donate once a week,

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.530
then they're being abstinent for two days before they go in.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.490
If they're trying to donate twice a week,

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:07.110
then they're abstinent for two days twice a week.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.110
And some of the donors I interviewed tried to donate three times a week.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.660
So then they're really sort of really scheduling their sex lives in a way

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.320
that many of them did find somewhat onerous.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:16.890
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And I should say

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.300
that although we're using the term donation, that's

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.280
the common term, as both of us have written about in our work,

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.130
there's lots of controversy about whether that's the right term.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.420
Some people prefer provision, which is more neutral.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.674
Some people like to say sale, because it is kind of being compensated,

00:04:30.674 --> 00:04:32.590
at least the portion that we're talking about.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.430
And we'll keep using the word donation because it's more natural.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.680
But always good to kind of acknowledge the way

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.565
in which these terms carry meaning.

00:04:39.565 --> 00:04:41.440
DR. RENE ALMELING: It's an interesting point,

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.400
and there has been a lot of debate in the literature as you note.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.860
For me as a sociologist, I'm really interested in how this industry thinks

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:50.360
of itself and how it works.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:54.150
And so it's important to me that the term that they use is donation.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.110
And then of course we can sit here and say,

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.460
well you're being paid for donation, and a paid donation

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.080
seems a little oxymoronic.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.260
But I think it's important to understand why they use that language,

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.010
how that language functions in organizing the market

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.110
and shaping donors experiences.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:10.580
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And this leads into the question

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:11.840
about how men are recruited.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:13.780
So how are sperm donors recruited?

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.450
What's the messaging about sperm donation?

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.214
Tell me a little bit more about that.

00:05:18.214 --> 00:05:21.380
DR. RENE ALMELING: Well historically, sperm banks really relied on this idea

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.560
that sperm donation was this easy thing.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:26.410
It's something that's a little bit funny.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:28.310
They make jokes in their advertisements.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:32.030
And so the advertisements really focus on cartoon images of sperm.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:35.280
There might be some small print about helping somebody to become a family.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.950
But the focus is really on, come in and get

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:40.820
paid for something you're already doing, to quote one of the ads.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.470
So they draw on these sort of cheeky advertisements in order to recruit men

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.650
in, because they're dealing with the college age population for the most

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:48.150
part.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.530
But donation is not really framed for men

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:55.590
as something that is important or really contributing to the lives of others

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:56.950
by helping them have families.

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.784
The focus really is on, come in and provide some sperm and get paid.

00:05:59.784 --> 00:06:02.366
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: So let's talk a little about the way

00:06:02.366 --> 00:06:05.110
in which these men are recruited and in particular,

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.530
sort of gendered messaging in recruitment

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.329
and the conceptions of employment.

00:06:09.329 --> 00:06:11.620
This is a really important part of your work right now.

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.869
DR. RENE ALMELING: Really the main argument of my book

00:06:13.869 --> 00:06:18.850
is that the language of donations suffuses both the world of egg agencies

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.220
and sperm banks.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.690
But in sperm banks a paid donation is understood as a job.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:25.730
They think of men as employees.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:28.012
They cut them paychecks every two weeks.

00:06:28.012 --> 00:06:29.720
They talk to them about this is something

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:32.840
they need to show up for on a regular basis, clock in and clock out,

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.300
and produce a passable sample.

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.060
And in egg donation, that paid donation is really

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:41.510
understood as a gift from one woman to another,

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:44.780
and that it's a gift in the service of creating a family.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.600
So even though this language of donation is

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.050
similar across the sperm donation and egg donation, what the donation is

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.390
looks really different.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.680
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: How long do men typically stay sperm donors?

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.822
DR. RENE ALMELING: We actually don't have any data to answer that question.

00:06:58.822 --> 00:07:01.530
So one of the things that I think we'll probably talk about later

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.740
is the fact that this market is completely unregulated in the United

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:06.240
States.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.850
We have no idea how many donors there are,

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.020
how often they donate, how many children are born.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.370
So those basic questions, we actually don't have the answers to.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.970
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And let me just emphasize that last point

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.261
you said that we don't know how many children are born.

00:07:19.261 --> 00:07:20.490
There are no regulations.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.090
So in theory, there is no limit to the number of children that

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.650
could be born from a certain donor.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:28.660
Now, many of the clinics will say that they're responsible about this.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.200
They keep registries, although there's some question about whether people can

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:33.010
go to more than one registry, right?

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:34.051
DR. RENE ALMELING: Right.

00:07:34.051 --> 00:07:37.030
Well, sperm banks do ask their clients to report back

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:39.550
if they have a pregnancy, if they give birth to a child,

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:43.750
so that they can keep track of how many offspring have been born per donor.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.810
But they don't have any way of enforcing that reporting.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.500
And there's also no standards or regulations

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.010
for what the maximum is for that family.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:55.350
So we now have websites like DonorSiblingRegistry.com

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:58.590
where people can go and see that particular donor

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.440
from a particular sperm bank has 40 offspring who have registered just

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:06.390
with that website, or 75 or 175.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.520
So we do have some of these anecdotal reports

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.840
of very large numbers of children being born from particular donors.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.740
And that to me is one of the major things

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.700
that we do need to rectify in this market.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:17.600
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: One of the concerns

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.516
is what is sometimes called accidental incest, that you might actually

00:08:20.516 --> 00:08:23.790
end up having a romantic relationship with a half sibling at least, right?

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:24.790
DR. RENE ALMELING: Yeah.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:28.380
I think that is definitely one of the theoretical concerns that

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.360
has been out there.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:32.230
I'm actually more concerned about the health effects.

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:34.710
Because a lot of these donors are young college students

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:39.190
and they may have genetic mutations that will cause disease later in life

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:40.655
that they may not know about.

00:08:40.655 --> 00:08:42.530
And so there have been cases such as one that

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.550
was reported in The Journal of the American Medical Association

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.980
with a donor who didn't realize that he had this heart condition that

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:49.760
was genetic.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.730
It was a heritable condition.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:52.840
He was a sperm donor.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.490
He had 22 children, and six or seven of those kids

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:00.290
inherited that mutation, including a toddler who died from it.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:03.390
So in old fashioned, normal reproduction,

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:07.790
you know men could certainly have two or five or even eight or 10 children,

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:10.610
but when we ramp up the numbers as we have

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:14.770
with sperm donation, those unknown health risks that get passed on,

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.950
get passed on to a potentially much larger number of children.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:18.360
PROFESSOR I. GLENN COHEN: And we'll actually

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:21.150
talk about a case later in the course about a child who

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:24.320
inherits polycystic kidney disease and tries

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:29.440
to track down the identity of the sperm donor for that purpose.

